1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
The first fighter planes and deep water submarines.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:06,000
The earliest tanks.

3
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:09,000
The first large scale use of chemical weapons.

4
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:12,000
When industrial age technology and war first mixed on a large

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:15,000
scale, the end result was ruthlessly efficient

6
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:16,000
destruction.

7
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, World War I Tech on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
World War I exposed the dark side of the Industrial

9
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:45,000
Revolution. The technological achievements

10
00:00:45,001 --> 00:00:51,000
that created mass production, new forms of transportation,

11
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:55,000
and countless inventions were now being put to use with

12
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:58,000
with devastating efficiency on the battlefield.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,340
The development between 1865 and 1914 was remarkable.

14
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:08,920
Steel technology, explosive technology,

15
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:12,920
chemical technology, aviation, all of these elements

16
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:17,460
added to what happened in World War I.

17
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:24,000
Tanks, airplanes, guns, submarines,

18
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:28,300
and chemical warfare were all either created or refined in the

19
00:01:28,301 --> 00:01:31,200
wake of the Industrial Revolution.

20
00:01:31,201 --> 00:01:33,740
The creation of the assembly line resulted in fast and

21
00:01:33,741 --> 00:01:36,240
efficient mass production.

22
00:01:36,241 --> 00:01:38,540
Even human reproduction increased.

23
00:01:38,541 --> 00:01:41,660
Advances in hygiene and living standards sent populations

24
00:01:41,661 --> 00:01:44,960
skyrocketing throughout the late 19th century.

25
00:01:44,961 --> 00:01:48,160
The growth of population and the growth of weapons

26
00:01:48,161 --> 00:01:51,200
potential meant that there was carnage on a level previously

27
00:01:51,201 --> 00:01:53,540
unanticipated in any war.

28
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:58,500
You had more casualties in one battle during the World War I

29
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:00,740
than all of the casualties on both sides

30
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:03,740
in the American Civil War.

31
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:05,340
The carnage was huge.

32
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:08,160
Something on the order of 8 million soldiers were killed

33
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:11,560
and 12 million civilians were killed.

34
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:14,660
But this carnage didn't spring from nowhere.

35
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:17,960
The stage for conflict was set some 40 years before the first

36
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:20,060
shot was ever fired.

37
00:02:20,061 --> 00:02:23,300
in a series of European alliances.

38
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:26,100
Territorial disputes and geographic vulnerabilities

39
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:29,400
drove countries to commit to war if any member of an alliance

40
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,800
was attacked.

41
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:34,680
An alliance linked Austria-Hungary with Germany.

42
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:38,220
Tiny Serbia allied with Russia, as did France.

43
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:40,220
To balance the powers on the continent,

44
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:43,020
England joined Russia and France.

45
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:45,320
Everybody was lined up on one side or another,

46
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:48,320
and all it took was a single spark to unite the war.

47
00:02:48,321 --> 00:02:52,920
That spark on June 28, 1914 was the assassination

48
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,320
by a Bosnian Serb of Archduke Franz Ferdinand,

49
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:59,560
heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne.

50
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:02,900
The insult of having this man assassinated

51
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:08,280
ignited a war between Austro-Hungarian Empire and Serbia.

52
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:10,600
As Germany joined Austria-Hungary,

53
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:12,340
so Russia backed Serbia.

54
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:16,380
Within days, France and England had entered the fray.

55
00:03:16,381 --> 00:03:19,620
But Europe was ill-prepared for the consequences of what would

56
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:22,620
become its first large-scale modern war.

57
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:28,920
Neither alliance was able to sustain a mobile offensive

58
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:32,620
against the other after the first few months of the war.

59
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:36,040
So both sides dug in along the Western Front near the northeast

60
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:40,240
border of France, stretching 500 miles from Switzerland to the

61
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:43,840
English Channel. A system of parallel trenches

62
00:03:43,841 --> 00:03:46,480
came to symbolize this paralysis.

63
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:51,040
It became both home and grave to many stationed there.

64
00:03:51,140 --> 00:03:53,920
Modern war is now recognized.

65
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:55,780
It's a killing machine.

66
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:57,580
A lot of people are going to get killed,

67
00:03:57,680 --> 00:03:59,960
and it's for naught.

68
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:01,920
You're going to settle down into a stalemate.

69
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:05,220
Neither side will be able to achieve a decisive victory

70
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:07,800
over the other, and it will be a war of attrition,

71
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:09,560
and the last guy standing will win.

72
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:10,960
But what's he won?

73
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:13,840
Clifford Holliday joined up as a bugle boy

74
00:04:13,841 --> 00:04:18,580
16 years old. At 105, he was the last surviving

75
00:04:18,581 --> 00:04:22,480
World War I combat veteran from Canada.

76
00:04:22,481 --> 00:04:25,880
I think of it back now as a shadow.

77
00:04:25,881 --> 00:04:28,580
I know it's there. I know it happened.

78
00:04:28,581 --> 00:04:31,200
I know it's real. There's a good chance

79
00:04:31,201 --> 00:04:34,500
you're not coming back. There's a fairly good chance

80
00:04:34,501 --> 00:04:38,440
you're not coming back. I spent my 17th birthday

81
00:04:38,441 --> 00:04:42,440
in the front line traches.

82
00:04:42,441 --> 00:04:45,940
One of the most innovative of all World War I technologies

83
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:48,940
was the tank. The creation of the tank was a

84
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:53,380
direct response to the stalemate of trench warfare.

85
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:56,680
6,000 miles of the roughly 9-foot wide and 7-foot deep

86
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:59,500
trenches traced the western front.

87
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,500
Barbed wire was strewn in front of the trenches, and sandbags

88
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:06,400
sometimes provided a protective barrier.

89
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:10,100
Machine gun nests bristled with sprays of bullets.

90
00:05:10,101 --> 00:05:13,600
In the desolate ribbon of no man's land between the belligerents

91
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:17,200
were bomb craters, tree stumps, burning villages,

92
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:20,040
and the bodies of soldiers from both sides.

93
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:22,780
The challenge was to find something that would just

94
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:23,940
plow through them.

95
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:27,320
The tank was the answer.

96
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:29,480
There are antecedents from the tank.

97
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:33,920
Da Vinci has drawings, which are these war machines, he calls

98
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:35,420
them.

99
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:37,420
But they didn't have the technology until they married

100
00:05:37,421 --> 00:05:41,420
the idea of the Holt tractor, which was a tracked vehicle,

101
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:46,520
up with an armored cab, and then drive it over the barbed wire

102
00:05:46,620 --> 00:05:49,900
and put machine guns or cannon on it to suppress the imaging

103
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,740
machine gunness.

104
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:53,900
The British quickly developed the Mark series

105
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:55,580
of tanks.

106
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:57,780
Beginning with the Mark I, the tank

107
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:01,280
applied a host of technologies, the internal combustion engine,

108
00:06:01,380 --> 00:06:03,880
steel plating, mounted machine guns,

109
00:06:03,881 --> 00:06:07,280
plus the all-important tracks instead of wheels.

110
00:06:07,380 --> 00:06:09,860
The tread is the key because it can go over terrain

111
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:11,760
that no wheel can go over.

112
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:13,160
It's harder to bog.

113
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:16,260
And the reason is it has a broad surface area,

114
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:20,260
so it has a very light pounds per square inch on the ground.

115
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:22,700
So in soft, muddy ground, it has a better chance

116
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:24,460
of staying afloat above the ground

117
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:26,400
rather than sinking in.

118
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:29,100
With a crew of six men, the early British tanks

119
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:31,880
could travel at about three miles an hour.

120
00:06:31,881 --> 00:06:35,020
Two men worked the delicate 100-horsepower engine,

121
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:38,620
which sat exposed inside the tank.

122
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:40,620
Though the British were quick to make improvements

123
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:43,720
on the Mark series tanks, they remained extremely

124
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:48,160
uncomfortable and even lethal inside throughout the war.

125
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:51,000
Temperatures could rise up over 100 degrees in there.

126
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:54,400
The stink of oil and gasoline and exhaust vapors

127
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:56,500
filled the inside of the vehicle.

128
00:06:56,600 --> 00:06:58,980
The engine is right inside the fighting compartment.

129
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:01,880
Driving this thing would be similar to driving

130
00:07:01,881 --> 00:07:05,880
if you sat underneath the hood with it closed next to the engine.

131
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:11,520
As machine gun bullets beat on the hull,

132
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:14,460
the sounds of that ringing, that cacophony,

133
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:16,520
is added to the roar of the motor.

134
00:07:16,620 --> 00:07:20,300
Worse yet, spall splashed off the inside of the hull

135
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:21,300
of the vehicle.

136
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:23,900
Fragments of the steel plating and paint

137
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,400
would cut the faces and the hands any exposed flesh

138
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:28,400
or the crewman to.

139
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:30,840
So now you've got a vehicle with no suspension system

140
00:07:30,841 --> 00:07:34,180
going 4 miles an hour, it's 120 inside and you're in leather.

141
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:36,180
So how long are you going to last?

142
00:07:36,280 --> 00:07:39,080
Well, the answer is probably not long.

143
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:42,580
So they had drawbacks to them.

144
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:46,360
However, given the alternative, there was no comparison.

145
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:49,900
In January of 1916, the British deployed the tank

146
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:51,600
for the first time.

147
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:55,400
The first tank attack in the Battle of the Somme,

148
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:57,640
36 tanks were put into place.

149
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:00,840
They did almost nothing except give away the idea.

150
00:08:00,841 --> 00:08:04,680
But a year later, at Cambrai, nearly 400 tanks

151
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:07,640
were put into effect, and they busted the German trenches,

152
00:08:07,740 --> 00:08:09,120
which showed what tanks could do.

153
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:13,580
They could bust the trenches, and they advanced 6,000 yards.

154
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:16,360
Meanwhile, the French, with the aid of the Renault

155
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:20,360
Company, were developing their own lighter tanks.

156
00:08:20,460 --> 00:08:23,500
At roughly 7 1⁄2 tons, they were a quarter the weight

157
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:24,800
of the heavy British tanks.

158
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:30,840
The standard French light tank was the standard

159
00:08:30,841 --> 00:08:34,080
It could be armed with a machine gun or a 37-millimeter gun.

160
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:35,320
It had two men inside.

161
00:08:35,420 --> 00:08:38,840
The communication between the tank commander gunner

162
00:08:38,940 --> 00:08:41,480
and the driver was a bit crude.

163
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:44,420
The way they did that was the tank commander

164
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:48,920
would kick the driver in the direction he wanted to go.

165
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:51,460
At six miles an hour, the French tanks

166
00:08:51,560 --> 00:08:53,200
were twice as fast as the British tanks.

167
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:56,440
They also incorporated a rotating turret.

168
00:08:56,540 --> 00:08:59,840
The French innovation of the rotating turret tank

169
00:08:59,841 --> 00:09:03,140
in a tank was extremely important.

170
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:05,840
Instead of having to have six or eight guns located all

171
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:09,380
around the surface of the tank to shoot from every angle,

172
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:12,480
you could have one gun that could shoot from every angle.

173
00:09:12,580 --> 00:09:16,420
One of only two surviving unmodified FT-18 French

174
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:20,320
tanks was recently discovered in a dump in Afghanistan,

175
00:09:20,420 --> 00:09:24,260
where it had likely been discarded in the 1920s.

176
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:27,200
It was donated to the George S. Patton Museum in Fort Knox,

177
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:28,900
Kentucky for restoration.

178
00:09:28,901 --> 00:09:35,000
This tank is operated by a series of control rods, a regular gas pedal, a regular clutch, and a regular shifter.

179
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:38,860
The turret of the tank was a one-man turret.

180
00:09:38,861 --> 00:09:41,000
It was traversed by the gunner.

181
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:44,020
It had no powered traverse mechanism.

182
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:48,140
He would just lean back into two shoulder mounts and turn the turret.

183
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:51,520
There are vision slits around the hatch.

184
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:54,400
This represents the crew's only way of seeing out of the tank.

185
00:09:54,401 --> 00:10:03,020
This is the entrance and exit from the turret, which would be immediately over the engine deck if the turret was installed.

186
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:07,640
This is the four-cylinder power plant that we removed from the tank.

187
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:11,280
It had a total power output of approximately 39 horsepower.

188
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:17,160
This engine represented state-of-the-art in internal combustion technology.

189
00:10:17,161 --> 00:10:19,160
The reliability of the tank,

190
00:10:19,260 --> 00:10:21,360
coupled with its reasonable ammunition supply,

191
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:23,900
would allow the tank to operate out to a radius

192
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:25,700
of about 35 miles.

193
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:29,140
This was far in excess of the larger tanks

194
00:10:29,240 --> 00:10:31,680
that were available during World War I.

195
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:33,740
Despite the effort the Allies put

196
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:37,120
into these mobile fortresses, the Germans lagged behind

197
00:10:37,220 --> 00:10:40,120
in designing and implementing tanks.

198
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:42,760
That was smart, because tanks are offensive weapons,

199
00:10:42,860 --> 00:10:44,780
and for the most part on the Western Front,

200
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:46,460
Germany was on the defense.

201
00:10:46,461 --> 00:10:48,260
in defense.

202
00:10:48,261 --> 00:10:52,760
World War I tanks were useless in defense.

203
00:10:52,761 --> 00:10:55,040
As significant a technological accomplishment

204
00:10:55,041 --> 00:10:58,140
as the tank was, its impact was limited,

205
00:10:58,141 --> 00:11:01,400
not because it wasn't effective, but because no one

206
00:11:01,401 --> 00:11:04,440
knew how to exploit it.

207
00:11:04,441 --> 00:11:08,040
Tactical use of the tank within a limited battle or skirmish

208
00:11:08,041 --> 00:11:11,480
hadn't yet been fully conceived because the tank was such a new

209
00:11:11,481 --> 00:11:14,180
concept.

210
00:11:14,181 --> 00:11:18,660
1918, with the end of the war in sight, did a coordinated tank

211
00:11:18,661 --> 00:11:22,660
assault contribute to pushing the Germans back out of France.

212
00:11:22,661 --> 00:11:26,660
Still, the tank was an important new weapon that held great

213
00:11:26,661 --> 00:11:29,620
promise for visionary military leaders.

214
00:11:29,621 --> 00:11:32,660
Combined with an infantry assault that could hold on to

215
00:11:32,661 --> 00:11:35,860
enemy territory, the tank's aggressive drive across the

216
00:11:35,861 --> 00:11:40,340
battlefield would prove not to be only tactical, but also

217
00:11:40,341 --> 00:11:45,140
strategic, decisive, not just in an individual battle,

218
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:48,540
but as part of an overall plan of attack to permanently defeat

219
00:11:48,640 --> 00:11:51,460
an enemy. But its greatest military

220
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:57,360
contribution would be realized in the next world conflict.

221
00:11:57,460 --> 00:12:00,320
Tank was actually the code word, short for water tank,

222
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:03,060
that the British used for early armored vehicles, such as the

223
00:12:03,160 --> 00:12:06,200
Mark I, produced in secret during the first years of the

224
00:12:06,300 --> 00:12:07,300
war.

225
00:12:10,340 --> 00:12:14,340
The first war in which air superiority played a role in

226
00:12:14,341 --> 00:12:19,340
tactical, if not strategic thinking, was World War I.

227
00:12:19,341 --> 00:12:22,340
The machines that led to this new concept of warfare included

228
00:12:22,341 --> 00:12:27,340
the observation balloon, the Zeppelin, and most importantly,

229
00:12:27,341 --> 00:12:31,340
the airplane, an engineering amalgam of aerodynamics and the

230
00:12:31,341 --> 00:12:36,340
internal combustion engine. Low-tech observation balloons

231
00:12:36,341 --> 00:12:40,780
Low-tech observation balloons up to 200 feet long and made of

232
00:12:40,781 --> 00:12:44,380
rubberized cotton could be sent up near the front.

233
00:12:44,381 --> 00:12:47,580
On a clear day, they could provide a 14-mile panorama

234
00:12:47,581 --> 00:12:51,280
and a view of enemy encampments.

235
00:12:51,281 --> 00:12:54,200
One or two observers could be positioned in the wicker basket

236
00:12:54,201 --> 00:12:57,600
below the balloon. They were especially used

237
00:12:57,601 --> 00:13:00,440
on the western front by the Germans, and very effectively

238
00:13:00,441 --> 00:13:02,540
so. They'd put them up behind the

239
00:13:02,541 --> 00:13:05,540
lines but high enough to where they could see the Allied line.

240
00:13:05,541 --> 00:13:08,440
lines without having to worry about flying over the lines.

241
00:13:08,441 --> 00:13:10,780
They were connected to the ground with telephone or

242
00:13:10,781 --> 00:13:14,880
telegraph lines so they could immediately communicate.

243
00:13:14,881 --> 00:13:17,680
Trench warfare effectively eliminated horse-mounted

244
00:13:17,681 --> 00:13:21,180
cavalry reconnaissance because there were no flanks to skirt

245
00:13:21,181 --> 00:13:26,100
in order to observe the enemy. The ability to view the enemy

246
00:13:26,101 --> 00:13:29,000
from an observation balloon was now vitally important.

247
00:13:31,940 --> 00:13:35,540
The Zeppelin was an improvement on the observation balloon.

248
00:13:35,541 --> 00:13:39,180
It was essentially a series of compartmentalized air sacs

249
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:42,880
filled with hydrogen, held together by an aluminum frame

250
00:13:42,980 --> 00:13:44,780
and attached to an engine.

251
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:48,380
It was maneuverable and capable of speeds approaching 70 miles

252
00:13:48,480 --> 00:13:49,680
an hour.

253
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:52,300
They were built in a way that if the engine cut out,

254
00:13:52,400 --> 00:13:54,600
a mechanic could crawl outside and work on the engine

255
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:55,800
and crawl back inside.

256
00:13:55,900 --> 00:13:58,200
It's every bit as complex as an ocean liner,

257
00:13:58,300 --> 00:14:01,540
but flies a whole lot higher.

258
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:04,540
The Zeppelin had been invented in 1900.

259
00:14:04,541 --> 00:14:07,680
By the start of the war, Germany had specialized combat

260
00:14:07,780 --> 00:14:09,280
zeppelins.

261
00:14:09,380 --> 00:14:11,640
They flew them at night and above the range of artillery

262
00:14:11,740 --> 00:14:13,180
fire.

263
00:14:13,280 --> 00:14:15,280
At first, they were just for reconnaissance.

264
00:14:15,380 --> 00:14:19,520
But in January 1915, the first zeppelin bombing run was made

265
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:20,720
on England.

266
00:14:20,820 --> 00:14:23,520
The bombing was inaccurate to the extreme.

267
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:26,300
There really was not an understanding of what aerodynamics

268
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:29,240
did to bombs as they fell, so they pretty much went wherever

269
00:14:29,340 --> 00:14:31,040
they wanted to.

270
00:14:31,140 --> 00:14:32,540
The zeppelins were never decisive.

271
00:14:32,541 --> 00:14:35,540
And in economic terms, they were a huge waste of money

272
00:14:35,640 --> 00:14:38,280
because they took an awful lot to build and maintain,

273
00:14:38,380 --> 00:14:40,880
and the results they returned were infinitesimal.

274
00:14:40,980 --> 00:14:43,380
They were also a danger to the crew.

275
00:14:43,480 --> 00:14:47,180
Zeppelins were full of buoyant but highly flammable hydrogen.

276
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:49,760
If something were to shoot the Zeppelin with an incendiary

277
00:14:49,860 --> 00:14:53,320
bullet, the whole thing could flame up and crash.

278
00:14:55,860 --> 00:14:58,860
The biggest threat to the Zeppelin would be the airplane,

279
00:14:58,861 --> 00:15:02,860
which would ultimately fly higher, faster, and better

280
00:15:02,861 --> 00:15:06,860
than any dirigible. It was just 10 years old and

281
00:15:06,861 --> 00:15:09,860
hardly more than a kite when the war began.

282
00:15:09,861 --> 00:15:12,860
But within four years, the airplane would be one of the

283
00:15:12,861 --> 00:15:15,860
greatest technological accomplishments to emerge from

284
00:15:15,861 --> 00:15:19,860
the war. The initial aircraft in World

285
00:15:19,861 --> 00:15:24,860
War I were flimsy, slow. They couldn't fly very high, and

286
00:15:24,861 --> 00:15:27,360
and they tended to fall apart.

287
00:15:27,361 --> 00:15:29,360
If you put them in a dive, the wing would come off.

288
00:15:29,361 --> 00:15:30,860
They just weren't very sturdy.

289
00:15:30,861 --> 00:15:33,860
But very quickly, as people saw the military capability,

290
00:15:33,861 --> 00:15:36,860
they started building sturdier aircraft with bigger engines

291
00:15:36,861 --> 00:15:40,860
that were more powerful that could carry weapons.

292
00:15:40,861 --> 00:15:43,860
Like balloons, planes were used mostly in conjunction

293
00:15:43,861 --> 00:15:46,860
with cameras and made their greatest wartime contribution

294
00:15:46,861 --> 00:15:47,860
as reconnaissance craft.

295
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:52,860
One of the earliest reconnaissance planes used

296
00:15:52,861 --> 00:15:57,860
used during World War I was the Blario, a single-wing,

297
00:15:57,861 --> 00:16:01,860
one-man plane with a 50-horsepower engine.

298
00:16:01,861 --> 00:16:06,860
The entire plane weighed around 800 pounds and had no weapons.

299
00:16:06,861 --> 00:16:10,860
Many early reconnaissance pilots were ex-cavalry, who no longer

300
00:16:10,861 --> 00:16:13,860
had as significant a role to play on the ground.

301
00:16:13,861 --> 00:16:16,860
They began taking rifles, pistols, up to shoot at one

302
00:16:16,861 --> 00:16:21,860
another, and began taking hand grenades, small bombs, artillery

303
00:16:21,861 --> 00:16:25,060
shells, even rocks to drop on people on the ground just

304
00:16:25,160 --> 00:16:26,860
because they were aggressive.

305
00:16:26,960 --> 00:16:29,460
This led ultimately to the development of specialized

306
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:32,700
fighting aircraft, some intended as bombers,

307
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:35,200
others intended as fighters.

308
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:36,800
The goal of the fighters was always

309
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:39,420
to shoot down the reconnaissance aircraft.

310
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:40,880
The challenge for a pilot was how

311
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:43,520
to shoot at a target without damaging his own plane.

312
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:47,960
A machine gun right in front of the pilot

313
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:51,220
provided easy aim down the nose of the plane.

314
00:16:51,221 --> 00:16:56,220
but resulted in a shot-off propeller.

315
00:16:56,221 --> 00:16:59,220
Side-mounted guns were another option, but they could be

316
00:16:59,221 --> 00:17:04,220
awkward and hard to aim. Finally, Dutch inventor Tony

317
00:17:04,221 --> 00:17:08,220
Fokker figured out a way to aim a gun down the nose and have it

318
00:17:08,221 --> 00:17:11,220
shoot through the path of the propeller without actually

319
00:17:11,221 --> 00:17:14,220
hitting the blades. The best solution is called

320
00:17:14,221 --> 00:17:17,220
an interrupter gear. As the propeller swings across

321
00:17:17,221 --> 00:17:20,220
the gun, it interrupts the machine gun from firing and

322
00:17:20,221 --> 00:17:23,820
until the propeller passes, and it fires again.

323
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:27,260
Fokker figured out a method that used a mechanical lever.

324
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:32,760
When the propeller spun across it, the gun stopped firing.

325
00:17:32,860 --> 00:17:35,900
The first true combat aircraft using Fokker's interrupter

326
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,180
gear was the German Fokker Eindecker,

327
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:41,980
a lightweight single-wing aircraft capable of speeds

328
00:17:42,080 --> 00:17:44,680
approaching 85 miles per hour.

329
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:47,580
Pursuit aircraft is what they call them.

330
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:49,680
Pursuit aircraft, all you got to do is

331
00:17:49,681 --> 00:17:51,540
is swing your airplane toward the enemy

332
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:53,120
and fire the machine gun.

333
00:17:53,220 --> 00:17:57,120
Now, this sounds romantic, but basically this is murder.

334
00:17:57,220 --> 00:17:59,820
What you're trying to do is sneak up on an enemy

335
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:01,220
while they're busy taking pictures

336
00:18:01,320 --> 00:18:03,820
and then shoot him in the back with your machine gun.

337
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:05,620
The men in the trenches grew familiar

338
00:18:05,720 --> 00:18:08,840
with the buzz of aircraft flying above the front.

339
00:18:08,940 --> 00:18:11,440
You see them up there fighting?

340
00:18:11,540 --> 00:18:13,540
Never fun with people shooting each other,

341
00:18:13,640 --> 00:18:18,680
but such as it was, it was a show of its own.

342
00:18:18,681 --> 00:18:21,780
like a bullfight in Mexico, I guess.

343
00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:24,980
Dog fights created a demand for stronger, lighter,

344
00:18:25,080 --> 00:18:26,820
faster air ground.

345
00:18:26,920 --> 00:18:29,820
Engineers looked to save an ounce of weight on an aircraft that

346
00:18:29,920 --> 00:18:32,620
was mostly canvas and wood.

347
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:34,520
The wings, for instance, on a lot of them

348
00:18:34,620 --> 00:18:37,900
are hollowed out pieces of wood.

349
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:42,400
And they would hollow out all the wooden frame numbers they

350
00:18:42,500 --> 00:18:44,740
could just to make them as light as they could.

351
00:18:44,840 --> 00:18:47,180
And you look at it initially, and you think they're only

352
00:18:47,181 --> 00:18:48,820
They're only saving ounces.

353
00:18:48,821 --> 00:18:50,320
But over the entire aircraft, they're

354
00:18:50,321 --> 00:18:53,020
saving several pounds, maybe even hundreds of pounds

355
00:18:53,021 --> 00:18:55,720
by the time it's all done.

356
00:18:55,721 --> 00:18:58,820
Plane engines also went through a swift evolution

357
00:18:58,821 --> 00:19:01,020
as pilots pushed their machines to heights

358
00:19:01,021 --> 00:19:03,540
approaching 25,000 feet.

359
00:19:03,541 --> 00:19:06,600
Just taking the engine from an 80-power engine that

360
00:19:06,601 --> 00:19:10,300
spins around and around and around to a huge 400-horsepower

361
00:19:10,301 --> 00:19:13,340
engine with 12 cylinders popping up and down inside,

362
00:19:13,341 --> 00:19:15,380
this is a major achievement.

363
00:19:15,381 --> 00:19:19,140
famous fighters was the Sopwith Camel, named for the slight hump

364
00:19:19,141 --> 00:19:23,140
visible near the cockpit. It had a 130-horsepower rotary

365
00:19:23,141 --> 00:19:27,140
engine with cylinders arranged in a circle rather than the more

366
00:19:27,141 --> 00:19:31,140
familiar straight line. Both the propeller blades and the

367
00:19:31,141 --> 00:19:34,340
engine rotated around the shaft of the propeller.

368
00:19:34,341 --> 00:19:37,140
This cooled the engine, but it created a tremendous

369
00:19:37,141 --> 00:19:40,580
gyroscope force. So the plane was enormously

370
00:19:40,581 --> 00:19:43,620
maneuverable if you turned in the direction of the spin of

371
00:19:43,621 --> 00:19:46,560
If you tried to turn the other way, it didn't do very well.

372
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:48,220
But if you were a superb pilot, it

373
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:50,360
was extraordinarily maneuverable.

374
00:19:50,460 --> 00:19:52,860
It needed to be because the German army

375
00:19:52,960 --> 00:19:54,360
had come up with what was probably

376
00:19:54,460 --> 00:19:57,560
the best aircraft of the war, the Fokker D7.

377
00:19:57,660 --> 00:20:01,600
It had a powerful Mercedes engine, two machine guns.

378
00:20:01,700 --> 00:20:04,680
Unlike most fighters, the wings were cantilevered, which

379
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:06,180
means self-supporting.

380
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:09,980
And they didn't need a lot of wires to hold them together.

381
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:12,340
The fast and agile Fokker D7 were

382
00:20:12,341 --> 00:20:15,680
more than any other plane, demonstrated the tactical

383
00:20:15,780 --> 00:20:19,940
possibilities of planes as offensive weapons.

384
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:23,920
Within the world of flying aces, it loomed large.

385
00:20:24,020 --> 00:20:26,120
But within the context of the entire war,

386
00:20:26,220 --> 00:20:28,720
it was not decisive.

387
00:20:28,820 --> 00:20:30,400
At the end of the First World War,

388
00:20:30,500 --> 00:20:32,400
the airplane's greatest contribution

389
00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:34,060
remained reconnaissance.

390
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:37,140
Probably the most significant use

391
00:20:37,240 --> 00:20:40,700
of aerial reconnaissance in the war

392
00:20:40,701 --> 00:20:44,500
in the war was in 1918 on the Allies' side.

393
00:20:44,501 --> 00:20:47,400
General Ludendorff launched the last German offensive,

394
00:20:47,401 --> 00:20:49,140
and it nearly succeeded.

395
00:20:49,141 --> 00:20:50,920
He was trying to win the war quickly

396
00:20:50,921 --> 00:20:53,740
before Americans got into the field in great numbers.

397
00:20:53,741 --> 00:20:57,540
Aerial reconnaissance let the Allies know what was going on,

398
00:20:57,541 --> 00:21:01,360
so they were able to attack where Germans had a weakness.

399
00:21:01,361 --> 00:21:04,200
The camera was the only strategic piece of equipment

400
00:21:04,201 --> 00:21:07,320
on a World War I airplane.

401
00:21:07,321 --> 00:21:09,320
But it would take the more sophisticated planes

402
00:21:09,321 --> 00:21:13,720
World War II for aircraft to play a bigger offensive role,

403
00:21:13,721 --> 00:21:16,920
controlling airspace and engaging in bombing raids

404
00:21:16,921 --> 00:21:19,260
against supplies and civilians.

405
00:21:22,460 --> 00:21:26,140
Few airplanes had radios during the First World War.

406
00:21:26,141 --> 00:21:28,640
The radio's bulky size and its inability

407
00:21:28,641 --> 00:21:31,240
to transmit extended-range voice signals

408
00:21:31,241 --> 00:21:32,340
made it impractical.

409
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:38,820
Despite cutting-edge machinery like airplanes and tanks,

410
00:21:38,821 --> 00:21:43,680
The weapons of the trenches were guns, artillery, and a host of small bombs.

411
00:21:44,700 --> 00:21:46,820
The machine gun was the most effective of all.

412
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:52,640
The machine gun, as were so many weapons used in World War I, was an American invention.

413
00:21:52,641 --> 00:21:57,360
The first effective automatic machine gun was the Maxim, which was designed by an American.

414
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:04,440
Irish immigrant and amateur inventor Hiram Maxim was reportedly moved to invent the machine gun in 1885

415
00:22:04,441 --> 00:22:07,700
after someone told him, if you want to make a lot of money,

416
00:22:08,280 --> 00:22:10,260
invent something that will enable these Europeans

417
00:22:10,261 --> 00:22:12,720
to cut each other's throats with greater facility.

418
00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:16,840
Maxim achieved his machine gun's rapid-fire ability,

419
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:19,460
using the thrust of the recoil

420
00:22:19,461 --> 00:22:21,760
to quickly pull the next bullet into the chamber.

421
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,800
He had limited success selling it to the American Army,

422
00:22:25,801 --> 00:22:27,960
so he went to Europe and sold it

423
00:22:27,961 --> 00:22:30,080
to just about every country over there.

424
00:22:30,460 --> 00:22:33,480
So many of the combatants were using Maxim guns

425
00:22:33,481 --> 00:22:36,380
designed based on the maximum.

426
00:22:36,480 --> 00:22:39,220
Another method of rapid fire was developed that collected

427
00:22:39,320 --> 00:22:42,920
the expanding gases, expelled when the gun was fired,

428
00:22:43,020 --> 00:22:46,820
to trigger a mechanism that allowed accelerated shooting.

429
00:22:46,920 --> 00:22:50,500
The best machine guns had a range of about 4,000 yards,

430
00:22:50,600 --> 00:22:53,360
with an aimed range of 1,000 yards shooting

431
00:22:53,460 --> 00:22:55,300
rifle caliber bullets.

432
00:22:55,400 --> 00:22:58,340
The battlefield is going to become dominated by a

433
00:22:58,440 --> 00:23:03,140
recoil-operated machine gun, which can spew out four

434
00:23:03,141 --> 00:23:06,140
400 to 500 rounds a minute.

435
00:23:06,240 --> 00:23:09,440
Not only that, but it has a water jacket on it,

436
00:23:09,540 --> 00:23:11,380
which keeps the barrel cool.

437
00:23:11,480 --> 00:23:14,980
So you can fire this thing and fire it and fire it and fire it

438
00:23:15,080 --> 00:23:18,080
and keep on firing.

439
00:23:18,180 --> 00:23:20,960
Almost as important as the gun's ability to fire rapidly

440
00:23:21,060 --> 00:23:23,260
was its ability to pivot.

441
00:23:23,360 --> 00:23:25,360
Guns were placed on a special mount,

442
00:23:25,460 --> 00:23:27,740
called a traversing and elevation mechanism,

443
00:23:27,840 --> 00:23:31,000
that enabled each gunner to spray rounds in an arc.

444
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:33,140
By swiveling, you could cover

445
00:23:33,141 --> 00:23:36,480
500 yards of battlefront with one machine gun.

446
00:23:36,580 --> 00:23:40,040
And if you could shoot out to 400 yards and men were walking

447
00:23:40,140 --> 00:23:42,980
across 400 yards, you could pretty much kill them all

448
00:23:43,080 --> 00:23:45,280
with one machine gun before they get anywhere near you.

449
00:23:45,380 --> 00:23:48,480
A typical heavy machine gun weighed about 50 pounds,

450
00:23:48,580 --> 00:23:51,400
with the mount adding another 70 pounds or so.

451
00:23:51,500 --> 00:23:53,500
It was not designed to be portable.

452
00:23:53,600 --> 00:23:56,140
It was designed to stay in the trenches.

453
00:23:56,240 --> 00:23:59,440
This is a very solid mount, so it's not like it's bucking

454
00:23:59,540 --> 00:24:01,440
around a lot when you're firing.

455
00:24:01,441 --> 00:24:04,340
The crew is about six people.

456
00:24:04,440 --> 00:24:07,440
You have ammo bearers.

457
00:24:07,540 --> 00:24:09,340
You have four people to carry the mount.

458
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:11,340
There's people to keep water in the water jacket

459
00:24:11,440 --> 00:24:13,180
to keep it from overheating.

460
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:15,780
And you have people who continually feed ammunition

461
00:24:15,880 --> 00:24:17,480
into these.

462
00:24:17,580 --> 00:24:21,500
Lighter portable machine guns were developed around 1915.

463
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:23,600
They still weighed around 30 pounds,

464
00:24:23,700 --> 00:24:25,900
with their mounts adding another 20 pounds.

465
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:29,100
A typical example is the French Hotchkiss.

466
00:24:29,101 --> 00:24:32,940
You can fire this thing practically forever.

467
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:36,600
Even though it's not water-cooled, it will get very hot.

468
00:24:36,700 --> 00:24:39,140
But these large fins here, which are basically

469
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:42,240
the feature of the Hotchkiss machine gun,

470
00:24:42,340 --> 00:24:45,520
puts enough area out that this thing will get red,

471
00:24:45,620 --> 00:24:46,780
guarantee you.

472
00:24:46,880 --> 00:24:49,480
But it will continue to shoot.

473
00:24:49,580 --> 00:24:51,520
By far the least effective machine gun

474
00:24:51,620 --> 00:24:56,460
was the French-designed lightweight, air-cooled Chauchat.

475
00:24:56,461 --> 00:25:00,040
And when you're trying to fire it, with this long recoil,

476
00:25:00,140 --> 00:25:01,140
it just shakes itself.

477
00:25:01,720 --> 00:25:05,540
There is aluminum around the barrel to help in the air cooling.

478
00:25:05,660 --> 00:25:08,580
They figured the air holes would help pass air down it.

479
00:25:08,581 --> 00:25:11,680
But all it ends up doing is jamming the weapon

480
00:25:11,681 --> 00:25:12,880
by getting more dirt in there.

481
00:25:13,860 --> 00:25:17,620
Because the magazines are so curved and open,

482
00:25:17,800 --> 00:25:20,060
you get dirt and everything else inside here.

483
00:25:21,160 --> 00:25:24,160
Most people considered them the weapon from hell.

484
00:25:24,161 --> 00:25:27,660
Despite technological failures like the Chauchat,

485
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:30,000
the guns were killing machines.

486
00:25:30,100 --> 00:25:32,900
Old school generals ordered their soldiers to charge,

487
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,500
only to have the men mowed down.

488
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:38,240
In virtually every battle of World War I,

489
00:25:38,340 --> 00:25:41,380
the machine gun played a decisive role.

490
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:45,520
It was particularly decisive in the early British

491
00:25:45,620 --> 00:25:46,520
offensives.

492
00:25:46,620 --> 00:25:49,880
In the Somme, for instance, this is where thousands

493
00:25:49,980 --> 00:25:52,380
and thousands of British soldiers were killed daily

494
00:25:52,381 --> 00:25:54,620
were killed daily for six months.

495
00:25:54,621 --> 00:25:57,120
And they were just charging machine guns and being mowed

496
00:25:57,121 --> 00:25:59,220
down because the British generals didn't have a better

497
00:25:59,221 --> 00:26:00,420
idea.

498
00:26:00,421 --> 00:26:05,520
As Winston Churchill said, there was something illogical about

499
00:26:05,521 --> 00:26:11,340
trying to attack machine guns with the chests of soldiers.

500
00:26:11,341 --> 00:26:13,840
As devastating as the machine gun was,

501
00:26:13,841 --> 00:26:16,980
large caliber guns, collectively known as artillery,

502
00:26:16,981 --> 00:26:20,480
were even more destructive, wreaking havoc with explosions

503
00:26:20,481 --> 00:26:24,980
and machinery into the air. Artillery weapons can be divided

504
00:26:24,981 --> 00:26:28,480
into three types. The first is a gun that shoots line

505
00:26:28,481 --> 00:26:31,480
of sight. The shell is fairly heavy, so the

506
00:26:31,481 --> 00:26:34,480
explosive power that shoots it out must be strong.

507
00:26:34,481 --> 00:26:37,480
Howitzers shoot at a high angle, and they generally don't shoot

508
00:26:37,481 --> 00:26:41,480
as far. The explosive force launching the shell is lighter.

509
00:26:41,481 --> 00:26:44,480
Therefore, the actual shell itself can have a thinner wall

510
00:26:44,481 --> 00:26:48,480
with more explosives and can do more damage.

511
00:26:48,481 --> 00:26:51,780
Mortars are like small howitzers that shoot up at an angle,

512
00:26:51,880 --> 00:26:54,620
with shells that come down almost vertically.

513
00:26:54,720 --> 00:26:57,220
They tend to carry considerable explosive power

514
00:26:57,320 --> 00:26:59,220
for their size.

515
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:01,220
Even before the trenches were dug,

516
00:27:01,320 --> 00:27:03,520
artillery was pummeling defensive structures

517
00:27:03,620 --> 00:27:05,640
along the Belgian border.

518
00:27:05,740 --> 00:27:07,940
Belgium hoped to rely on a group of fortresses

519
00:27:08,040 --> 00:27:10,640
to prevent the Germans from invading.

520
00:27:10,740 --> 00:27:13,180
But Germany had developed massive guns,

521
00:27:13,280 --> 00:27:16,540
nicknamed Big Bertha's, which were some of the largest

522
00:27:16,541 --> 00:27:20,480
of the largest caliber artillery pieces built during the war.

523
00:27:20,580 --> 00:27:23,240
Some of these explosive packed shells weighed over 2,000

524
00:27:23,340 --> 00:27:24,680
pounds.

525
00:27:24,780 --> 00:27:27,580
And some of these guns were so massive that they could only

526
00:27:27,680 --> 00:27:31,780
be transported in pieces by rail car.

527
00:27:31,880 --> 00:27:35,400
Belgium's fortresses fell within an afternoon.

528
00:27:35,500 --> 00:27:38,800
These big guns literally smashed those fortresses

529
00:27:38,900 --> 00:27:43,900
flat, which allowed the German army to go through Belgium.

530
00:27:43,901 --> 00:27:47,600
And no one had ever seen modern fortifications smashed up

531
00:27:47,700 --> 00:27:49,040
like that.

532
00:27:49,140 --> 00:27:51,800
Though of smaller caliber than the Big Bertha's,

533
00:27:51,900 --> 00:27:54,140
the most technologically innovative of all the siege

534
00:27:54,240 --> 00:27:58,720
mortars was the Paris gun, built to terrify Parisian civilians

535
00:27:58,820 --> 00:28:01,080
into surrendering.

536
00:28:01,180 --> 00:28:05,080
The 210-millimeter gun's 131-foot long steel

537
00:28:05,180 --> 00:28:10,260
barrel could hurl shells over 10 miles high into the stratosphere,

538
00:28:10,261 --> 00:28:14,500
and the gun could be fired from over 75 miles away.

539
00:28:14,600 --> 00:28:16,100
The French didn't know what they were being hit with.

540
00:28:16,200 --> 00:28:18,300
They thought they were being bombed by a Zeppelin,

541
00:28:18,400 --> 00:28:21,340
or a gas works had blown up, or something like that.

542
00:28:21,440 --> 00:28:24,340
Then once they found out that these explosions were

543
00:28:24,440 --> 00:28:26,440
occurring in sort of a straight line,

544
00:28:26,540 --> 00:28:28,420
oh, it was a gun.

545
00:28:28,520 --> 00:28:31,340
More than 256 Parisians were killed

546
00:28:31,440 --> 00:28:33,820
in a barrage that lasted for six months

547
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:37,080
and dropped 351 shells.

548
00:28:37,180 --> 00:28:38,980
But the Paris gun was too unwieldy

549
00:28:38,981 --> 00:28:41,980
a consistent barrage. The barrel needed constant

550
00:28:41,981 --> 00:28:45,980
replacement, and it was impossible to target accurately.

551
00:28:45,981 --> 00:28:49,980
In the end, it cost Germany manpower and resources that it

552
00:28:49,981 --> 00:28:53,980
couldn't spare. Trench mortars, however,

553
00:28:53,981 --> 00:28:56,980
were particularly effective along the front.

554
00:28:56,981 --> 00:28:59,980
They were essentially just a strong tube in which an explosive

555
00:28:59,981 --> 00:29:04,980
shell was inserted. Braced against the ground,

556
00:29:04,981 --> 00:29:08,580
They needed no recoil absorbing mechanism.

557
00:29:08,680 --> 00:29:12,180
Mortars like the Stokes 3-inch and the Newton 6-inch

558
00:29:12,280 --> 00:29:15,520
threw explosive shells, gas shells, flares,

559
00:29:15,620 --> 00:29:17,920
or even shrapnel up into the air

560
00:29:18,020 --> 00:29:20,620
to drop nearly vertically and unobstructed

561
00:29:20,720 --> 00:29:22,640
into an enemy trench.

562
00:29:22,740 --> 00:29:26,740
The key is that trench mortars were ones that were small enough

563
00:29:26,840 --> 00:29:28,740
to be moved into the trenches.

564
00:29:28,840 --> 00:29:32,040
And they had a short range, 500, 800 yards,

565
00:29:32,041 --> 00:29:34,680
but enough to hit the trench on the other side,

566
00:29:34,780 --> 00:29:36,780
and there was really no defense against it.

567
00:29:36,880 --> 00:29:38,340
The only defense was to dig a hole

568
00:29:38,440 --> 00:29:41,080
and hope that they didn't drop a shell right in it.

569
00:29:41,180 --> 00:29:43,120
Coordinated with machine guns,

570
00:29:43,220 --> 00:29:45,820
trench mortars were lethally effective.

571
00:29:45,920 --> 00:29:48,660
Machine gun spray could herd men into clusters.

572
00:29:48,760 --> 00:29:53,560
Then mortars could literally rain death down on them.

573
00:29:53,660 --> 00:29:55,960
But both the Central and the Allied powers

574
00:29:56,060 --> 00:29:59,640
were developing another weapon that would prove both physically

575
00:29:59,740 --> 00:30:02,040
and psychologically effective.

576
00:30:02,041 --> 00:30:06,280
a weapon that was nearly invisible and lighter than air.

577
00:30:09,480 --> 00:30:12,280
Although the flamethrower was developed and used during the

578
00:30:12,281 --> 00:30:15,280
First World War, it was a technology that made no

579
00:30:15,281 --> 00:30:18,800
strategic contribution. It frequently malfunctioned,

580
00:30:18,801 --> 00:30:22,200
often enveloping the two-man crew in flames.

581
00:30:26,600 --> 00:30:30,800
Chemical weapons were by far the most insidious technology of the

582
00:30:30,801 --> 00:30:32,800
of the First World War.

583
00:30:32,900 --> 00:30:36,540
Large-scale chemical warfare began in World War I,

584
00:30:36,640 --> 00:30:39,540
but the concept was around much, much earlier.

585
00:30:39,640 --> 00:30:41,540
Even during the American Civil War,

586
00:30:41,640 --> 00:30:45,140
there were concepts of employing a chlorine-type shell.

587
00:30:45,240 --> 00:30:48,620
But each time, the concept was rejected because it was thought

588
00:30:48,720 --> 00:30:51,580
to be against warfare.

589
00:30:51,680 --> 00:30:55,960
Germany had no such qualms during the First World War.

590
00:30:56,060 --> 00:30:59,200
There was the 1899 Hague Convention

591
00:30:59,201 --> 00:31:02,440
that said you cannot use a shell, that the sole purpose

592
00:31:02,540 --> 00:31:05,100
was to disseminate a toxic gas.

593
00:31:05,200 --> 00:31:07,840
So to get around that agreement, they

594
00:31:07,940 --> 00:31:11,140
mixed a high explosive with a chemical agent.

595
00:31:11,240 --> 00:31:13,880
Early on, both sides attempted to lace grenades

596
00:31:13,980 --> 00:31:17,620
with non-lethal harassing agents, like tear gas.

597
00:31:17,720 --> 00:31:19,780
Tear gas is known as a lacrimate,

598
00:31:19,880 --> 00:31:23,060
which means it irritates the tear ducts, causing them to tear

599
00:31:23,160 --> 00:31:26,900
and obstruct vision. But any chemical agent must gently

600
00:31:26,901 --> 00:31:29,660
the air to be effective.

601
00:31:29,760 --> 00:31:32,700
The first use of chemical warfare in World War I

602
00:31:32,800 --> 00:31:34,200
was tear gas.

603
00:31:34,300 --> 00:31:37,640
The Germans used it first, but the French didn't notice.

604
00:31:37,740 --> 00:31:40,140
I guess the wind was too high.

605
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:42,140
The Germans then used it against the Russians.

606
00:31:42,240 --> 00:31:43,140
It was too cold.

607
00:31:43,240 --> 00:31:44,660
The tear gas froze.

608
00:31:44,760 --> 00:31:47,060
After these false steps, the German military

609
00:31:47,160 --> 00:31:50,060
intensified its efforts, developing lethal gas

610
00:31:50,160 --> 00:31:52,400
for use in mortar shells.

611
00:31:52,500 --> 00:31:55,060
It was aided by a chemist named Fritz Haber.

612
00:31:55,061 --> 00:31:55,960
Haber.

613
00:31:55,961 --> 00:31:59,160
Haber was a leading scientist and academic whose work in chemistry

614
00:31:59,161 --> 00:32:00,960
was world-renowned.

615
00:32:00,961 --> 00:32:04,960
A deeply patriotic Prussian, he pioneered the use of chlorine,

616
00:32:04,961 --> 00:32:07,000
and later, phosgene, as a weapon.

617
00:32:07,001 --> 00:32:10,700
CHLORINE AND PHOSGENE ARE RESPIRATORY AGENTS.

618
00:32:10,701 --> 00:32:14,420
They attack the lungs, and the victims wind up actually

619
00:32:14,421 --> 00:32:18,120
drowning in their own bodily fluids.

620
00:32:18,121 --> 00:32:20,520
The Germans first used chlorine on the battlefield

621
00:32:20,521 --> 00:32:24,420
of Ypres, Belgium, in 1915, as they were attempting to push

622
00:32:24,421 --> 00:32:26,420
through the British and French lines.

623
00:32:26,520 --> 00:32:29,120
They waited until the wind was blowing toward the French

624
00:32:29,220 --> 00:32:33,420
to prevent any gas from floating back over their own troops.

625
00:32:33,520 --> 00:32:38,060
The first attack was very psychologically effective

626
00:32:38,160 --> 00:32:40,660
in that the average French soldier

627
00:32:40,760 --> 00:32:45,180
did not know what to expect and saw this cloud coming

628
00:32:45,280 --> 00:32:46,180
towards them.

629
00:32:46,280 --> 00:32:49,280
At first, they thought maybe the German trenches were on fire

630
00:32:49,380 --> 00:32:50,980
and there was just smoke.

631
00:32:50,981 --> 00:32:55,620
But once they smelled it and got gassed by it and got affected by it,

632
00:32:55,920 --> 00:32:57,580
the word spread very quickly.

633
00:32:58,620 --> 00:33:01,860
Terrified soldiers fled to escape the sinister yellow cloud

634
00:33:01,861 --> 00:33:03,680
as it floated toward their position.

635
00:33:04,360 --> 00:33:07,640
The gas may have cost the British as many as 5,000 lives.

636
00:33:08,400 --> 00:33:10,960
But the Germans did not capitalize on their success

637
00:33:10,961 --> 00:33:14,020
because they failed to follow up on the attack.

638
00:33:14,780 --> 00:33:18,220
The Allies ultimately blocked their advance and pushed them back.

639
00:33:18,221 --> 00:33:23,720
Shortly after Ypres, both sides began hurling chemical agents at each other.

640
00:33:23,721 --> 00:33:29,220
It was a chemical arms race as each side attempted to develop more effective weapons.

641
00:33:29,221 --> 00:33:34,220
But launching enough poison to create a cloud sufficient to saturate the air to lethal levels

642
00:33:34,221 --> 00:33:40,220
was a technological challenge. 75 and 155 millimeter artillery shells

643
00:33:40,221 --> 00:33:45,220
were long range and carried a lot of agent into the trenches, but it could take hundreds

644
00:33:45,221 --> 00:33:49,220
to create a cloud. Once again, chemist Fritz Haber

645
00:33:49,221 --> 00:33:53,220
offered his expertise. Fritz Haber decided that one

646
00:33:53,221 --> 00:33:59,220
way was to simply use the chlorine in a chemical cylinder.

647
00:33:59,221 --> 00:34:03,220
Attackers could launch 20 to 50 times more gas in the cylinder

648
00:34:03,221 --> 00:34:06,220
than they could inside the much smaller shell, which also had

649
00:34:06,221 --> 00:34:10,220
explosives taking up space inside it.

650
00:34:10,221 --> 00:34:13,220
Launching the cylinders was effective, but it was a clear

651
00:34:13,221 --> 00:34:16,860
But it was a clear violation of the Hague Convention.

652
00:34:16,960 --> 00:34:19,520
Clifford Holliday remembers the first time he was gassed

653
00:34:19,620 --> 00:34:21,720
during a battle.

654
00:34:21,820 --> 00:34:24,260
It was a foggy night.

655
00:34:24,360 --> 00:34:27,060
And when they turned that gas loose,

656
00:34:27,160 --> 00:34:29,460
it just stayed right on the ground.

657
00:34:29,560 --> 00:34:32,740
But by 1916, Holliday and his comrades

658
00:34:32,840 --> 00:34:34,740
were now equipped, however poorly,

659
00:34:34,840 --> 00:34:37,520
to counter the effects of the creeping gas cloud.

660
00:34:37,620 --> 00:34:42,320
The first gas mask we had was a piece of square chamois

661
00:34:42,321 --> 00:34:48,660
soaks in this chemical, and it was in a rubber-lying bag

662
00:34:48,661 --> 00:34:52,360
with a zipper on it, and when you run into that,

663
00:34:52,361 --> 00:34:54,360
you just hang it over your nose.

664
00:34:54,361 --> 00:34:56,360
Ammonia and other chemicals in the cloth

665
00:34:56,361 --> 00:34:59,280
neutralized the acid passing through.

666
00:34:59,281 --> 00:35:03,280
Within a short time, however, defense technology improved,

667
00:35:03,281 --> 00:35:06,980
providing soldiers with masks that included charcoal filters

668
00:35:06,981 --> 00:35:12,320
carried on the back that neutralized the poison.

669
00:35:12,321 --> 00:35:14,460
This is one of the later gas masks.

670
00:35:14,560 --> 00:35:17,760
The design had this hood that fit over the face,

671
00:35:17,860 --> 00:35:20,260
had the lenses that protect the eyes,

672
00:35:20,360 --> 00:35:23,160
and this hose which attached to a canister.

673
00:35:23,260 --> 00:35:28,100
This is the pack that carried it, and inside were a metal

674
00:35:28,200 --> 00:35:29,200
canister.

675
00:35:29,300 --> 00:35:34,240
The material that filtered the air was in this canister.

676
00:35:34,340 --> 00:35:36,380
But the bulky gas masks seriously

677
00:35:36,480 --> 00:35:38,280
reduced a soldier's effectiveness,

678
00:35:38,380 --> 00:35:40,720
making it awkward to aim a gun.

679
00:35:40,721 --> 00:35:44,060
And filtering agents did nothing to protect against so-called

680
00:35:44,160 --> 00:35:46,660
mustard gas, which wasn't a gas at all,

681
00:35:46,760 --> 00:35:49,360
but a sticky, persistent liquid.

682
00:35:49,460 --> 00:35:51,860
Mustard gas is a blister agent.

683
00:35:51,960 --> 00:35:54,960
It won't necessarily kill you, but it will rot your flesh,

684
00:35:55,060 --> 00:35:58,380
and it's also a pretty horrible thing.

685
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:01,880
After mustard gas was introduced in 1917,

686
00:36:01,980 --> 00:36:04,380
soldiers had to be covered in waterproof clothing

687
00:36:04,480 --> 00:36:06,220
that protected their skin.

688
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:08,880
That prevented exposure to the non-lethal,

689
00:36:08,881 --> 00:36:11,880
but extremely painful mustard agent.

690
00:36:11,980 --> 00:36:14,420
All of this was difficult on the troops,

691
00:36:14,520 --> 00:36:17,520
but gas technology didn't add up to much strategically.

692
00:36:17,620 --> 00:36:19,720
Gas played no decisive role in any battles

693
00:36:19,820 --> 00:36:22,120
of the First World War.

694
00:36:22,220 --> 00:36:25,400
Gas masks kept most from dying, and subject

695
00:36:25,500 --> 00:36:29,240
to meteorological whims, gas was simply too unreliable.

696
00:36:29,340 --> 00:36:31,540
It just made everybody more miserable

697
00:36:31,640 --> 00:36:34,140
and had some casualties, but didn't...

698
00:36:34,240 --> 00:36:35,880
It was not decisive.

699
00:36:35,980 --> 00:36:38,880
Though gas injured more than a million soldiers,

700
00:36:38,881 --> 00:36:42,680
it killed fewer than 100,000, representing less than 1%

701
00:36:42,681 --> 00:36:46,180
of the war's death toll. Germany's Fritz Haber is not

702
00:36:46,181 --> 00:36:49,920
listed among those 100,000, but it's hard not to think of him,

703
00:36:49,921 --> 00:36:53,120
the father of chemical weapons, as a victim of his own

704
00:36:53,121 --> 00:36:57,040
misguided patriotism. His wife, who was also a chemist,

705
00:36:57,041 --> 00:36:59,540
was so horrified by what he did that she committed suicide.

706
00:36:59,541 --> 00:37:03,040
She couldn't live with it. And the ultimate irony was he was

707
00:37:03,041 --> 00:37:06,480
run out of Germany in 1933 when Hitler took over because he was

708
00:37:06,481 --> 00:37:09,240
because he was Jewish and the Germans had no use for him

709
00:37:09,241 --> 00:37:09,941
anymore.

710
00:37:13,920 --> 00:37:15,920
A young Adolf Hitler was hospitalized

711
00:37:15,921 --> 00:37:19,220
during the First World War after being temporarily blinded

712
00:37:19,221 --> 00:37:20,620
by a poison gas attack.

713
00:37:28,840 --> 00:37:30,640
The development of the submarine

714
00:37:30,641 --> 00:37:33,240
may have been the most important technological advance

715
00:37:33,241 --> 00:37:36,180
of World War I. But though the submarine

716
00:37:36,181 --> 00:37:39,140
evolved into a formidable weapon during the war.

717
00:37:39,141 --> 00:37:42,480
Early examples had existed for nearly 200 years.

718
00:37:42,481 --> 00:37:45,780
You can look at a wide variety of water vehicles

719
00:37:45,781 --> 00:37:47,640
that can be called submersibles, all the way back

720
00:37:47,641 --> 00:37:51,040
to the Turtle, which attempted an attack on a British ship

721
00:37:51,041 --> 00:37:52,820
during the Revolutionary War.

722
00:37:52,821 --> 00:37:56,880
Robert Fulton pioneered some steam propelled variations

723
00:37:56,881 --> 00:37:59,120
on the submarine during the 19th century.

724
00:37:59,121 --> 00:38:01,340
And the Europeans did so as well.

725
00:38:01,341 --> 00:38:03,140
But the father of the modern submarine

726
00:38:03,141 --> 00:38:07,040
American named John Holland, who during the 1880s perfected a

727
00:38:07,041 --> 00:38:10,440
way to make a vessel easily submersible and truly

728
00:38:10,441 --> 00:38:15,880
maneuverable underwater. Holland used tanks of water and

729
00:38:15,881 --> 00:38:18,680
compressed air to raise and lower this up.

730
00:38:22,620 --> 00:38:25,500
He created a company which still builds submarines today,

731
00:38:25,501 --> 00:38:28,740
the Electric Boat Works. He sold them to England.

732
00:38:28,741 --> 00:38:32,440
The design was widely copied. Initially, they were powered by

733
00:38:32,441 --> 00:38:35,400
engines while on the surface, and electric battery-driven

734
00:38:35,401 --> 00:38:39,480
engines underwater. Two different types of engines

735
00:38:39,481 --> 00:38:41,780
were needed because internal combustion engines required

736
00:38:41,781 --> 00:38:44,780
oxygen and could only be used on the surface.

737
00:38:44,781 --> 00:38:48,200
Battery-powered electric engines did not need oxygen, so

738
00:38:48,201 --> 00:38:52,200
were used underwater. Gasoline engines were initially

739
00:38:52,201 --> 00:38:54,940
used for the surface engine, but they were prone to explosion

740
00:38:54,941 --> 00:38:57,100
in the confines of a small vessel.

741
00:38:57,101 --> 00:38:59,900
The noxious gases that they emitted made the living

742
00:38:59,901 --> 00:39:02,560
the living quarters all but uninhabitable.

743
00:39:02,660 --> 00:39:06,140
What they gradually discovered was that the diesel engine

744
00:39:06,240 --> 00:39:10,340
and the fact that diesel oil is less immediately combustible

745
00:39:10,440 --> 00:39:12,940
than gasoline, the diesel engine was a far,

746
00:39:13,040 --> 00:39:16,660
far better propulsion source for surface propulsion,

747
00:39:16,760 --> 00:39:18,760
and that they would use batteries for submerged

748
00:39:18,860 --> 00:39:19,760
propulsion.

749
00:39:19,860 --> 00:39:21,160
And of course, while they were on the surface,

750
00:39:21,260 --> 00:39:22,260
they could recharge their batteries

751
00:39:22,360 --> 00:39:24,960
and put in the diesel engines.

752
00:39:25,060 --> 00:39:27,260
Although it was an Irish-born American who developed

753
00:39:27,261 --> 00:39:30,160
modern submarine. It was Germany that established

754
00:39:30,161 --> 00:39:33,160
itself as a leader in sub-technology throughout World

755
00:39:33,161 --> 00:39:37,160
War I. Germany sought to counter Britain's

756
00:39:37,161 --> 00:39:41,200
superior navy by going underwater, developing an

757
00:39:41,201 --> 00:39:45,820
Untersee boat, or U-boat. Initially, submarines stayed

758
00:39:45,821 --> 00:39:49,820
relatively close to the surface and near ports, but changes in

759
00:39:49,821 --> 00:39:54,160
strategy drove them great distances, at 200 to 250 feet

760
00:39:54,161 --> 00:39:57,220
below the surface, moving at speeds of eight knots

761
00:39:57,300 --> 00:40:00,700
underwater and approaching 15 knots on the surface,

762
00:40:00,760 --> 00:40:03,600
making them more aggressive weapons.

763
00:40:03,660 --> 00:40:07,000
By 1918, the Germans had a fleet of 44 U-boats

764
00:40:07,080 --> 00:40:11,520
of various sizes, from 24-person short-range coastal subs

765
00:40:11,580 --> 00:40:17,120
up to massive 1,500-ton U cruisers with a crew of 60.

766
00:40:17,180 --> 00:40:19,820
The Germans also worked to perfect the torpedo,

767
00:40:19,880 --> 00:40:22,920
a weapon that would make their U-boats lethal.

768
00:40:22,921 --> 00:40:26,020
Compressed air or heated gases were used to power a turbine

769
00:40:26,120 --> 00:40:27,820
inside the torpedo.

770
00:40:27,920 --> 00:40:30,020
The torpedo improved its speed and accuracy

771
00:40:30,120 --> 00:40:32,120
over the course of the war.

772
00:40:32,220 --> 00:40:35,160
The turbines enabled torpedoes to extend themselves,

773
00:40:35,260 --> 00:40:38,860
achieving greater range and speeds of up to 24 knots,

774
00:40:38,960 --> 00:40:42,380
or roughly 35 miles per hour at longer distances.

775
00:40:42,480 --> 00:40:44,980
They were packed with TNT, or gun cotton,

776
00:40:45,080 --> 00:40:48,280
and could be set for depth and proper direction.

777
00:40:48,380 --> 00:40:52,180
Submarines also had deck guns for targets

778
00:40:52,181 --> 00:40:55,480
were either alone and unable to defend themselves,

779
00:40:55,580 --> 00:40:58,320
and it was cheaper to shoot them with an artillery cannon

780
00:40:58,420 --> 00:41:01,180
and sink them that way than it was to expend a torpedo,

781
00:41:01,280 --> 00:41:02,220
which was fairly expensive.

782
00:41:04,920 --> 00:41:07,820
U-Boats sank more than 6,500 Allied ships

783
00:41:07,920 --> 00:41:10,840
over the course of the war.

784
00:41:10,940 --> 00:41:15,540
One lone U-Boat sank 224 ships.

785
00:41:15,640 --> 00:41:17,440
But the key to the submarine's advantage

786
00:41:17,540 --> 00:41:20,340
was not its firepower.

787
00:41:20,341 --> 00:41:22,840
Its primary weapon was stealth.

788
00:41:22,940 --> 00:41:25,180
It couldn't be seen.

789
00:41:25,280 --> 00:41:28,580
You gave that up only at your own peril.

790
00:41:28,680 --> 00:41:30,620
The biggest danger a U-boat faced

791
00:41:30,720 --> 00:41:33,620
was a depth charge, a bomb set to go off

792
00:41:33,720 --> 00:41:36,260
at a certain depth underwater.

793
00:41:36,360 --> 00:41:39,400
The Allies attempted to use depth charges against U-boats,

794
00:41:39,500 --> 00:41:41,600
but their stealthiness made this a challenge.

795
00:41:45,440 --> 00:41:48,100
But the Germans weren't satisfied sinking only Allied

796
00:41:48,200 --> 00:41:49,340
ships.

797
00:41:49,341 --> 00:41:51,340
They realized that if they were to win the war,

798
00:41:51,440 --> 00:41:54,340
they needed to strategically attack supply ships,

799
00:41:54,440 --> 00:41:57,040
even if they were from so-called neutral countries.

800
00:42:00,020 --> 00:42:01,680
There was also a major debate, you know,

801
00:42:01,780 --> 00:42:03,580
if they shouldn't be torpedoing these vessels,

802
00:42:03,680 --> 00:42:05,580
because they are commercial vessels, after all.

803
00:42:05,680 --> 00:42:08,300
The German says, right, what you're bringing in on these

804
00:42:08,400 --> 00:42:10,100
vessels are going to be shot at me later on,

805
00:42:10,200 --> 00:42:12,300
because we are bringing in war materials.

806
00:42:12,400 --> 00:42:15,600
So as far as we're concerned, these are warships.

807
00:42:15,700 --> 00:42:19,340
Logistical targeting of supply ships nearly won the war

808
00:42:19,341 --> 00:42:22,040
for the Central Powers, as the Allies were cut off

809
00:42:22,041 --> 00:42:25,740
from outside supplies. But it angered neutral countries,

810
00:42:25,741 --> 00:42:29,340
like the United States. America finally entered the war

811
00:42:29,341 --> 00:42:34,080
on the Allied side, after German U-boats sank a number of American

812
00:42:34,081 --> 00:42:37,800
and other non-combat ships and passenger liners.

813
00:42:37,801 --> 00:42:40,360
The most famous of these, the British passenger liner

814
00:42:40,361 --> 00:42:43,460
Lusitania, was almost certainly smuggling materiel

815
00:42:43,461 --> 00:42:46,040
for the Allies. The use of unrestricted

816
00:42:46,041 --> 00:42:48,940
warfare brought America into the war.

817
00:42:49,040 --> 00:42:52,840
And the fresh soldiers who arrived in France in 1917

818
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:57,780
and 1918 were the decisive margin of victory for the Allies.

819
00:42:57,880 --> 00:43:00,480
So the weapon that almost won them the war ultimately

820
00:43:00,580 --> 00:43:02,280
cost them the war.

821
00:43:02,380 --> 00:43:04,600
Though both sides realized the same advantage

822
00:43:04,700 --> 00:43:07,100
from the new technologies, and the soldiers certainly

823
00:43:07,200 --> 00:43:10,240
helped, it was American manufacturing production

824
00:43:10,340 --> 00:43:12,840
that pushed the Allies over the edge.

825
00:43:12,841 --> 00:43:15,780
American raw materials were virtually limitless,

826
00:43:15,880 --> 00:43:18,640
and American supplies enabled the Allies to outlast

827
00:43:18,740 --> 00:43:20,140
their exhausted enemies.

828
00:43:20,240 --> 00:43:22,280
Because World War I was a laboratory

829
00:43:22,380 --> 00:43:24,680
for destructive technology, warfare

830
00:43:24,780 --> 00:43:28,780
changed drastically between 1914 and Armistice,

831
00:43:28,880 --> 00:43:32,800
November 11, 1918.

832
00:43:32,900 --> 00:43:35,700
Even as the soldiers were celebrating the Armistice,

833
00:43:35,800 --> 00:43:38,300
the dark clouds of the next conflict were already

834
00:43:38,400 --> 00:43:39,300
gathering.

835
00:43:42,840 --> 00:43:46,740
Some historians claim that World War II was simply a continuation

836
00:43:46,741 --> 00:43:50,540
of one great global war that began in 1914.

837
00:43:55,680 --> 00:43:59,080
What is undeniable is that the seeds of technically efficient

838
00:43:59,081 --> 00:44:02,600
destruction planted during the First World War would bear

839
00:44:02,601 --> 00:44:06,900
bitter fruit in the coming conflict, and on an even larger

840
00:44:06,901 --> 00:44:07,900
scale.

